   "question": "<p>Compute the following limit: $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^2\\sin^2(x)}$.</p>",
   "hints": [
    "<p>Use the Taylor expansion of $\\cos t$ around $0$ with $t=x^2$, and the fundamental limit $\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\to1$.</p>"
   ],
   "solution": "<p>Write\n\\[ \\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^2\\sin^2 x}=\\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^4}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sin x}\\right)^2. \\]\nBy the Maclaurin expansion $\\cos t=1-\\frac{t^2}{2}+o(t^2)$, and with $t=x^2$: $1-\\cos(x^2)=\\frac{x^4}{2}+o(x^4)$, hence $\\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^4}\\to\\frac12$.\n(Alternatively: $1-\\cos(x^2)=2\\sin^2(\\frac{x^2}{2})$, hence $\\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^4}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\sin(x^2/2)}{x^2/2}\\right)^2\\to\\frac12$.)\nAlso $\\frac{x}{\\sin x}\\to 1$ by the fundamental limit. By the arithmetic of limits:\n\\[ \\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1-\\cos(x^2)}{x^2\\sin^2 x}=\\frac12\\cdot 1=\\boxed{\\frac12}. \\]</p>",
   "src": "Moed B, 2015/16, Semester B",
   "exam": "2015/16 Semester B Moed B"
