   "question": "<p>Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\\[ \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot y' = y^2 \\arcsin x \\]</p>",
   "hints": [
    "<p>The equation is separable. After separating, on the $x$ side substitute $t = \\arcsin x$.</p>"
   ],
   "solution": "<p>The equation is defined for $-1 &lt; x &lt; 1$, and there $\\sqrt{1 - x^2} &gt; 0$. Write it in the form\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dx} = y^2 \\cdot \\frac{\\arcsin x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}, \\]\nwhich is a separable equation.</p>\n<p><strong>The constant solution:</strong> $y \\equiv 0$ satisfies the equation ($0 = 0$), so it is a solution.</p>\n<p><strong>For $y \\ne 0$:</strong> separate the variables:\n\\[ \\int \\frac{dy}{y^2} = \\int \\frac{\\arcsin x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\,dx. \\]\nThe left-hand side gives $-\\frac{1}{y}$. On the right-hand side substitute $t = \\arcsin x$, $dt = \\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}$:\n\\[ \\int \\frac{\\arcsin x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\,dx = \\int t\\,dt = \\frac{t^2}{2} + C = \\frac{\\arcsin^2 x}{2} + C. \\]\nHence\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{\\arcsin^2 x}{2} + C \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{-1}{\\frac{\\arcsin^2 x}{2} + C} = \\frac{-2}{\\arcsin^2 x + 2C}. \\]</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the general solution is\n\\[ \\boxed{y = \\frac{-2}{\\arcsin^2 x + C}}, \\quad C \\in \\R \\]\n(on every interval on which the denominator does not vanish), together with the solution $y \\equiv 0$.</p>",
   "src": "Moed B, 2025/26, Semester A",
   "exam": "2025/26 Semester A Moed B"
